<interrogationes unitas="color">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I turn a Color into a pixel for a TabulaPixelorum and back?
    <responsum symbolum="color_ad_pixelum color_ex_pixelum Color">color_ad_pixelum
      packs it as a &lt;&lt; 24 | b &lt;&lt; 16 | g &lt;&lt; 8 | r (ABGR
      in the 32-bit word), which on this little-endian machine lies in
      memory as the bytes R, G, B, A - the same layout as an Imago.
      color_ex_pixelum unpacks it. A literal written as 0xRRGGBBAA is
      NOT a pixel: build it with color_ex_rgba and convert.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I blend or fade between two colours?
    <responsum symbolum="color_interpolate">color_interpolate(a, b, t)
      with t from 0 (all a) to 256 (all b), applied to all four
      channels including alpha, in signed integer arithmetic that
      truncates toward zero. t is not clamped: outside 0..256 the
      result is cast to i8 and wraps. It mixes the sRGB bytes directly;
      for a perceptually even mix go through linear light.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why should colour mixing for dithering or gradients happen in linear light?
    <responsum symbolum="color_octetus_ad_linearem color_srgb_ad_linearem color_linearis_ad_srgb" via="lib/color.worklog.md">sRGB
      bytes are gamma-encoded: a black and white checkerboard looks like
      sRGB 188, not 128. Convert bytes with color_octetus_ad_linearem
      (a 256-entry table filled on first use), average there, and go
      back with color_linearis_ad_srgb, which returns 0..1 (multiply by
      255 and round yourself - there is no helper back to a byte). The
      conversions do not clamp values outside 0..1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I find which palette colour is perceptually closest to a given colour?
    <responsum symbolum="color_ad_oklab color_oklab_distantia color_linearis_ad_oklab" via="lib/dithering_exemplaria.c">Convert
      both to OKLab with color_ad_oklab (sRGB bytes) or
      color_linearis_ad_oklab (linear floats; negative channels clamp to
      0) and compare with color_oklab_distantia, a plain Euclidean
      distance: black to white is about 1.0. Alpha is ignored. The
      dithering_exemplaria library precomputes palette OKLab values and
      distances this way.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I round-trip linear RGB through OKLab exactly?
    <responsum symbolum="color_linearis_ad_oklab color_oklab_ad_linearem" via="lib/color.worklog.md">Not
      bit-exactly: Ottosson's published 10-digit matrices are not exact
      inverses, and the round trip errs by up to about 2.6e-7 (tests
      use 1e-6). That is far below 8-bit precision. color_oklab_ad_linearem
      does not clamp, so an OKLab point outside the gamut comes back
      with channels below 0 or above 1.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what does color_ad_cinereum compute?
    <responsum symbolum="color_ad_cinereum">An
      ITU-R BT.601 luma from the raw sRGB bytes, in integers: (30 R + 59
      G + 11 B) / 100, truncated, alpha ignored. It is not a linear-light
      luminance; for perceptual lightness use the L of
      color_ad_oklab.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can color_ex_palette read colours from the current theme palette?
    <responsum symbolum="color_ex_palette thema_palette_aquinas thema_ponere_palette">No:
      it always reads the fixed Aquinas palette from thema_palette_aquinas
      (16 entries of 6-bit components, scaled by CCLV / LXIII, alpha
      CCLV), whatever thema_ponere_palette set. The index is not checked:
      16 or more reads past the 48-byte table.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
